[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     6[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `c_src/include/driver.h`) for every rejection construct:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     7[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     8[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```sh
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     9[0m:[38;5;10m[49m grep -nE 'return|assert|NULL|errno|RETURN_ERROR|-1|if |switch|#if|MAX|MIN' \
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    10[0m:[38;5;10m[49m      src/driver.c include/driver.h
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    11[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    12[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    13[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Non-comment hits: only `#include <stdio.h>`, `#include <string.h>` and the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    14[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `DRIVER_H_` include guard. The complete body of the library is:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    15[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    16[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```c
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    17[0m:[38;5;10m[49m void driver(const char *s1, const char *s2) {
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    18[0m:[38;5;10m[49m     printf("%zu\n", strcspn(s1, s2));
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    19[0m:[38;5;10m[49m }
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    20[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    21[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    22[0m:[38;5;10m[49m So, factually: **the C library contains zero error-return statements, zero
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    23[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `assert`s, zero explicit range checks, zero null checks, zero error enums, and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    24[0m:[38;5;10m[49m zero min/max constants.** `driver` returns `void`, so it has no error channel at
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    25[0m:[38;5;10m[49m all, and it never inspects its arguments before handing them to `strcspn`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    26[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    27[0m:[38;5;10m[49m That makes the real rejection surface the *implicit* one: the preconditions
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    28[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `strcspn` imposes on its two arguments. Violating them is undefined behaviour in
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    29[0m:[38;5;10m[49m C, and the way the as-compiled C library actually behaves is the ground truth
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    30[0m:[38;5;10m[49m the Rust must reproduce. The rows below are those implicit rejections, each one
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    31[0m:[38;5;10m[49m established empirically against the built `libdriver.so` (a `driver` call in a
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    32[0m:[38;5;10m[49m forked child; `139` = killed by `SIGSEGV`).
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    33[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    34[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Two structural facts about glibc's `strcspn` drive most rows, and both were
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    35[0m:[38;5;10m[49m confirmed by probing the compiled `.so`:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    36[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    37[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * **R-ORDER**: the reject set `s2` is read *before* `s1` is touched at all
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    38[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   (glibc's generic path tests `reject[0]`/`reject[1]` first; the x86-64 SSE4.2
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    39[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   path tests `*a == 0` first).
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    40[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * **R-WHOLE**: the *entire* reject set is consumed before `s1` is scanned, even
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    41[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   when `s1[0]` matches `s2[0]` — glibc builds its full lookup table / SIMD mask
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    42[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   from `s2` up front, so an unterminated `s2` faults even when a naive
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    43[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   short-circuiting implementation would have returned early.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    44[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    45[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## The table
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    46[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    47[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | # | function | trigger (the exact invalid input/condition) | expected C result |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    48[0m:[38;5;10m[49m |---|----------|---------------------------------------------|-------------------|
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    49[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | E1 | `driver` | `s2 == NULL`, `s1` a valid non-empty string (`driver("abc", NULL)`) | SIGSEGV (exit 139), no output |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    50[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | E2 | `driver` | `s2 == NULL`, `s1` a valid **empty** string (`driver("", NULL)`) — per R-ORDER the empty `s1` does *not* save the call | SIGSEGV (exit 139), no output |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    51[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | E3 | `driver` | `s2` points at an unmapped (`PROT_NONE`) page, `s1` a valid empty string | SIGSEGV (exit 139), no output |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    52[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | E4 | `driver` | `s2` points at an unmapped page, `s1` a valid non-empty string | SIGSEGV (exit 139), no output |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    53[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | E5 | `driver` | `s1 == NULL`, `s2` a valid multi-character reject set (`driver(NULL, "abc")`) | SIGSEGV (exit 139), no output |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    54[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | E6 | `driver` | `s1 == NULL`, `s2` a valid **single**-character reject set (`driver(NULL, "a")`) — glibc's `reject[1]=='\0'` fast path | SIGSEGV (exit 139), no output |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    55[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | E7 | `driver` | `s1 == NULL`, `s2` a valid **empty** reject set (`driver(NULL, "")`) — glibc's `*a == 0` fast path, degenerates to `strlen(s1)` | SIGSEGV (exit 139), no output |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    56[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | E8 | `driver` | `s1 == NULL` **and** `s2 == NULL` | SIGSEGV (exit 139), no output |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    57[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | E9 | `driver` | `s1` points at an unmapped page, `s2` a valid non-empty reject set | SIGSEGV (exit 139), no output |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    58[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | E10 | `driver` | `s1` points at an unmapped page, `s2` a valid empty reject set | SIGSEGV (exit 139), no output |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    59[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | E11 | `driver` | `s1` valid, `s2` **unterminated**: reject bytes run straight into an unmapped page, and `s1[0]` matches `s2[0]` (a short-circuiting implementation would return `0` without faulting) — per R-WHOLE | SIGSEGV (exit 139), no output |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    60[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | E12 | `driver` | `s1` **unterminated**: `s1`'s bytes are all outside the reject set and run straight into an unmapped page, `s2` valid | SIGSEGV (exit 139), no output |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    61[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | E13 | `driver` | `s1` unterminated running into an unmapped page, `s2` the **empty** reject set (`strlen`-equivalent path) | SIGSEGV (exit 139), no output |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    62[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    63[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### Generic FFI boundary conditions (no dedicated C check exists, covered anyway)
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    64[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    65[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `driver` takes no integer, length, enum or flag parameter — its signature is
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    66[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `void driver(const char *, const char *)` — so there is no length argument to
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    67[0m:[38;5;10m[49m pass as zero/oversized and **no enum parameter whose out-of-range integer value
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    68[0m:[38;5;10m[49m could be smuggled across the FFI boundary**. The only out-of-domain values
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    69[0m:[38;5;10m[49m reachable through this API are pointer values (rows E1–E13) and byte values
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    70[0m:[38;5;10m[49m inside the two strings. The latter are *not* errors — every one of the 255
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    71[0m:[38;5;10m[49m non-NUL byte values is a legal input — so they are covered as valid
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    72[0m:[38;5;10m[49m configurations in `CONFIGS.md` (rows C13–C16, which specifically pin down the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    73[0m:[38;5;10m[49m signed-`char` sign-extension hazard for bytes `0x80..=0xFF`).
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    74[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    75[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Zero length is likewise legal, not an error: an empty `s1` and/or an empty `s2`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    76[0m:[38;5;10m[49m are well-defined inputs, covered by `CONFIGS.md` rows C1–C4. Oversized length
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    77[0m:[38;5;10m[49m has no representation in this API. "One step past a documented valid range" maps
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    78[0m:[38;5;10m[49m onto the byte-value domain and the reject-set-size thresholds where glibc
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    79[0m:[38;5;10m[49m switches implementation strategy (`CONFIGS.md` rows C6–C9).
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    80[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
