[38;5;252m[1m## The divergence[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[38;5;10mc_src/src/driver.c[0m is one line: [38;5;10mprintf("%zu\n", strcspn(s1, s2))[0m. The Rust translation reimplemented [38;5;10mstrcspn[0m by hand, and its argument read order differed from the glibc [38;5;10mstrcspn[0m the C library actually links against:[0m[0m
[0m[0m
| input | C [38;5;10m.so[0m | Rust [38;5;10m.so[0m (before) |[0m[0m
|---|---|---|[0m[0m
| [38;5;10mdriver("", NULL)[0m | SIGSEGV | printed [38;5;10m0[0m |[0m[0m
| [38;5;10mdriver("", <PROT_NONE page>)[0m | SIGSEGV | printed [38;5;10m0[0m |[0m[0m
| [38;5;10ms2[0m unterminated, [38;5;10ms1[0][0m in reject set | SIGSEGV | printed [38;5;10m0[0m |[0m[0m
[0m[0m
glibc reads the reject set [3mfirst and in full[23m — its generic path tests [38;5;10mreject[0][0m/[38;5;10mreject[1][0m to pick a fast path, and it builds a complete lookup table / SIMD mask before touching [38;5;10ms1[0m. The Rust version scanned [38;5;10ms1[0m first and searched [38;5;10ms2[0m linearly per byte, so an empty [38;5;10ms1[0m short-circuited the call and a match at [38;5;10ms1[0][0m stopped the [38;5;10ms2[0m scan early. Both were confirmed by probing the compiled [38;5;10m.so[0m files, not inferred from glibc sources.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
Fixed in [38;5;10msrc/lib.rs[0m by building a 256-entry membership table from all of [38;5;10ms2[0m up front, then scanning [38;5;10ms1[0m. Return values are unchanged for every well-defined input; only the fault behaviour moved into agreement.[0m[0m
